Excited to share that I’ve successfully built and deployed a fully automated CI/CD workflow! This pipeline streamlines the build, tag, and push process for Docker images, ensuring seamless deployment—all powered by Jenkins and Docker Hub. 

🔹 Step-by-Step Breakdown 

✅ Step 1: Clone the Project Repository 

git clone <repository_url>
 
This repo contains the application code along with a well-defined Dockerfile for building the image. 

✅ Step 2: Install Docker Pipeline Plugin 
Installed the Docker Pipeline plugin in Jenkins to integrate Docker commands directly into the pipeline. 

✅ Step 3: Build the Docker Image 
Using the Jenkinsfile to trigger: 

docker build -t <image_name>:<tag> .
 

✅ Step 4: Push Docker Image to Docker Hub 
By integrating DockerRegistry within Jenkins Pipeline syntax, I securely configured Docker Hub credentials using Jenkins Credentials Manager, then executed: 

docker.withRegistry('https://lnkd.in/gkQgdtgx', 'docker-hub-creds') {
 docker.image('<image_name>:<tag>').push()
}
 

✅ Step 5: Deploy the Container 
Pulled the image and ran it to verify successful deployment: 

docker run -d --name <container_name> -p <host_port>:<container_port> <docker_hub_repo>:<tag>
 

✅ Step 6: Parameterizing Deployment Variables 
Instead of hardcoding values like image name, tag, and ports, I configured them as parameters in Jenkins: 
- Choice Parameters (Docker Hub Username) 
- String Parameters (Repo name, Tag, Container name, Port) 

Upon saving, I can build dynamically using Build with Parameters—giving full flexibility in deployment! 


